A||||2||Roy||Taking the 4cyl out from the top||||4||
Z||000000||Roy||03-31-2000||06:50 AM||||Hey everyone, clutch went out on my 4cyl today. I need this car on saturday. I'm staying home from work tommroww and pulling the engine from the top. I choose to do this because I don't have the time to build the platform to drop the craddle on, and to just change the clutch this way seems the easiest. I disconnected everything from the engine earlier tonight. I own a cherry picker, and was wondering where some of you put the chains on the 4cyl. I wanna know what the strongest points are and where I should link the chains to, to lift it out. Also, when removing it, all I have to unbolt is the lower engine mounts and the bellhousing right? Btw the craddle isn't going to fall when I do this right:P? hehe <P>Thanks in advance, <P>Roy||||reg||4
Z||000001||87ECO5||03-31-2000||09:02 AM||||I've got two "tie-downs" on my 2.5 - one above the pulleys, one above the coil packs, both at a level just below the valve cover:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiedown.jpg"> <P>I always thought all 2.5 engines had them and that the factory used them when assembling the car.<P>Am I mistaken?||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||03-31-2000||01:07 PM||||The factory assemble the engine by the "puff and stuff" method. The powertrain would be on the the cradle all hooked up and it would be on a pnematic jack that would raise up to install it. Than the workers would bolt it up. The hooks could be used also to support the engine when the tranny is taken out since the engine only has the mount and the torque strut. I have seen many set ups with a stand that sits on the strut towers with chains the hook up to the engine to keep it up.<P>If any engine can be pulled from the top its the 4 cyl. Nothing wrong with it, its just another way of doing the same thing. Good luck with your clutch!||||reg||1
